  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving the awareness on the benefits of ADR for the private sector</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The awareness of alternative dispute resolution mechanisms remains low, both with smallholder farmers but also with private sector actors. In 2022, the Government of Rwanda adopted a new Alternative Dispute Resolution (ADR)-policy. This raised a momentum on dialogue and implementation (both with state and non-state actors) around ADR-mechanisms. This project, operating as a component within a wider LAND-at-scale intervention, aims to improve the awareness of private sector actors of the benefits of ADR to benefit the business climate as well as ensure disputes are resolved in a just and equitable way. This will be done by organising workshops on the benefits of ADR for private sector development, particularly focussing on mediation skills. This will target multiple organisations including the Ministry of Justice, the Private Sector Federation (PSF), the Rwanda Bar Association (RBA), the Kigali International Arbitration Center (KIAC) and professional mediators. Secondly, the project will aim to train at least 90 PSF arbitrators focussing on all Presidents and Vice-presidents on ADR methodologies, in particular the use of mediation. After the two day trainings, PSF arbitrators will be better equipped to execute their services in a fair and efficient manner to the private sector such as businesses of all sizes including entrepreneurs, and various stakeholders in the economic landscape.</narrative>
  </description>
